  ['What is a contractual control agreement?','A written contract that gives someone control over the future sale or long lease of land they do not own: typically an option agreement, a conditional contract, a right of pre-emption, or a promotion agreement that lets the promoter direct or request a sale. From 6 April 2027 the rights in these contracts must be reported to HM Land Registry if they affect registered land in England and Wales.'],
  ['When do the Contractual Control Regulations come into force?','The Provision of Information (Contractual Control) (Registered Land) Regulations 2026 (SI 2026/615) were made on 8 June 2026 and come into force on 6 April 2027. HM Land Registry’s digital submission service opens the same day.'],
  ['Do agreements signed before April 2027 need to be reported?','Rights granted on or after 8 June 2026 and before 6 April 2027 must be reported before 6 October 2027. Rights granted before 8 June 2026 are not reportable on grant, but become reportable if they are assigned or varied in writing on or after 6 April 2027, or if an application is made for a notice or restriction to protect them.'],
  ['Who has to report a contractual control right?','The grantee: the party with the benefit of the right, usually the developer or promoter. The submission itself must be made digitally by an individually regulated conveyancer, such as a solicitor, licensed conveyancer or chartered legal executive who is personally regulated.'],
  ['What is the deadline for reporting?','60 days from the event that triggers the duty: the grant, an assignment, a written variation that changes the information, or the right ending. For rights granted in the transitional window (8 June 2026 to 5 April 2027), the deadline is 6 October 2027.'],
  ['What happens if a contractual control right is not reported?','Failing to provide the information without reasonable excuse, or knowingly or recklessly providing false or misleading information, is an offence under section 225 of the Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023. HM Land Registry will also not generally enter a notice or restriction protecting the right until the information has been provided.'],
  ['Will the contractual control register be public?','Yes. HM Land Registry expects to publish the contractual control dataset from April 2028, refreshed at least monthly. It will show the grantee, the type of right, its duration and the land affected. Dates and places of birth of individual grantors, and conveyancers’ registration numbers, are not published.'],
  ['Are options under 18 months exempt?','A right that can only last for less than 18 months is exempt. If the contract allows the period to be extended to 18 months or more, the exemption does not apply: HMLR’s scenarios guidance gives the example of a 12-month option with a 12-month extension, which may be reportable.'],
