    <div class="card"><p class="eyebrow">On this page</p><ol class="toc"><li><a href="#what">What the regime is</a></li><li><a href="#rights">The four rights caught</a></li><li><a href="#criteria">The five tests</a></li><li><a href="#exempt">Exemptions</a></li><li><a href="#who">Who reports</a></li><li><a href="#when">When: the triggers and deadlines</a></li><li><a href="#what-info">What HMLR asks for</a></li><li><a href="#consequences">If you don’t report</a></li><li><a href="#public">The 2028 dataset</a></li><li><a href="#now">What to do now</a></li></ol></div>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What the regime is</h2>
  <p>Most strategic land in England and Wales changes hands twice. First a developer or promoter takes <em>control</em> through a contract: an option, a conditional contract, a pre-emption right or a promotion agreement. Years later, if planning lands, the land is actually transferred. Until now only the second step was visible on the register. The first was private, protected at most by a notice that told the world something existed without saying who or for how long.</p>
  <p>Part 11 of the <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2023/55/part/11" rel="noopener">Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023</a> gave the government power to change that. The <a href="https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2026/615" rel="noopener">Provision of Information (Contractual Control) (Registered Land) Regulations 2026</a> (SI 2026/615) use it. They were made on 8 June 2026 and come into force on 6 April 2027. From then, whoever holds a qualifying right has to report it to HM Land Registry, through a regulated conveyancer, within 60 days of each event that triggers the duty.</p>
  <blockquote><p>The register does not change who owns the land. It makes visible who controls what happens to it next.</p></blockquote>
  <p>HM Land Registry published its operational guidance, <a href="https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/contractual-control-rights-pg-84/practice-guide-84-contractual-control-rights" rel="noopener">Practice guide 84</a>, and a set of <a href="/hmlr/contractual-control-scenarios/">worked scenarios</a> on 26 October 2026. Both are reflected below.</p>

  <h2 id="rights">The four rights caught</h2>
  <p>Practice guide 84 groups contractual control rights into four categories. The label in the contract does not matter; the substance of the right does.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>What it does</th><th>Typical agreement</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><a href="/agreements/option-agreements/">Option</a></td><td>Lets the holder require the owner to transfer the freehold or grant a long lease</td><td>Strategic land option, call option</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/agreements/conditional-contracts/">Conditional contract</a></td><td>A binding sale or lease that completes only if conditions are met, often planning</td><td>Subject-to-planning purchase</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/agreements/pre-emption-rights/">Pre-emption</a></td><td>A right of first refusal if the owner decides to sell</td><td>Right of first refusal, pre-emption agreement</td></tr>
      <tr><td><a href="/agreements/promotion-agreements/">Direct or request a sale</a></td><td>A right to require or request a sale or lease, often to a third-party buyer</td><td>Land promotion agreement</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>One contract can contain several rights. HMLR’s scenarios guidance is explicit that the Regulations apply to each right separately, so a promotion agreement with a fallback option may need two sets of information.</p>

  <h2 id="criteria">The five tests</h2>
  <p>A right is a contractual control right only if every one of these is true. Our <a href="/agreement-checker/">agreement checker</a> walks through them in order.</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>It is in a written contract.</strong> A deed counts. Statutory rights and rights ordered by a court do not.</li>
    <li><strong>It controls a future transfer or long lease of registered land.</strong> Freehold, or a lease of 15 years or more. Land that is wholly unregistered is outside the regime, as are options over only an easement.</li>
    <li><strong>The grantor is the registered owner.</strong> A right granted by someone who is not yet registered as owner is not reportable on grant (HMLR scenario 1), though it can become reportable later.</li>
    <li><strong>It is held for the purposes of an undertaking.</strong> Business, charitable and public-function purposes qualify. A right held for purely personal reasons, such as an option on your own future home, does not.</li>
    <li><strong>No exemption applies.</strong> See below, and read them narrowly: HMLR says they should be.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="exempt">Exemptions</h2>
  <p>Six categories take a right outside the regime. Each has an edge that catches people out; the full detail is in our <a href="/agreements/exemptions/">exemptions guide</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Short-term rights.</strong> A right that can only last for less than 18 months. If extensions could take it to 18 months or more, the exemption is lost.</li>
    <li><strong>Not for development.</strong> Rights held exclusively for a purpose unrelated to housing or development creating 100 square metres or more of floorspace. HMLR suggests an option bought as an investment may still be caught, because the value usually depends on future development.</li>
    <li><strong>Overage.</strong> Rights securing further payments to a former owner.</li>
    <li><strong>Finance security.</strong> Rights necessary or incidental to security for a loan or financial instrument.</li>
    <li><strong>Section 106 infrastructure.</strong> Rights relating exclusively to infrastructure, amenities or services linked to a planning permission. Affordable housing rights under a section 106 agreement are <em>not</em> exempt (HMLR scenario 8).</li>
    <li><strong>National security or defence.</strong></li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="who">Who reports</h2>
  <p>The duty sits with the <strong>grantee</strong>, the party with the benefit of the right. In practice that is the developer, housebuilder or promoter. A landowner indemnity does not move it. Anyone applying for a notice or restriction to protect a right must also make sure the information has been provided first.</p>
  <p>The submission itself has to be made digitally by an <a href="/hmlr/who-can-submit/">individually regulated conveyancer</a>: a solicitor, licensed conveyancer or chartered legal executive regulated in their own right. Unregulated staff at a regulated firm cannot submit, and nor can an in-house land team unless it has its own regulated conveyancer. The conveyancer gives their registration number and regulator; that is not published.</p>

  <h2 id="when">When: the triggers and deadlines</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Deadline</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Right granted 8 Jun 2026 to 5 Apr 2027</td><td>By 6 October 2027, reflecting any later changes</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Right granted on or after 6 Apr 2027</td><td>60 days from the grant</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Assignment to a new grantee</td><td>60 days, including rights granted before 8 June 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Written variation that alters the information</td><td>60 days, including rights granted before 8 June 2026</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Right ends: exercised, determined or expires</td><td>60 days, confirming how and whether wholly or in part</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Application for a notice or restriction</td><td>Before the application proceeds, whenever the right was granted</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>Two details matter. First, a right granted before 8 June 2026 is not reportable just because the regime starts; it is the next assignment or variation from 6 April 2027 that catches it. Second, where events overlap, one report can cover several if it is updated before the deadline. Work out any date with the <a href="/deadline-calculator/">deadline calculator</a>, and check a pre-April 2027 agreement with the <a href="/transitional-period-checker/">transitional period checker</a>. More in <a href="/compliance/60-day-deadline/">the 60-day rule</a> and <a href="/compliance/transitional-period/">the transitional period</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="what-info">What HMLR asks for</h2>
  <p>Section 7 of Practice guide 84 lists it. Most answers in the digital service will be multiple choice.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Full names of grantor and grantee; for companies and other entities, the registration number, issuing authority and country of incorporation; for an individual grantor, date and place of birth (not published).</li>
    <li>The type of right, from the four categories.</li>
    <li>The contract: its date, parties and title or description.</li>
    <li>When the right can be exercised, or the conditions (not needed for pre-emption).</li>
    <li>The initial period of control, from contract date to the latest exercise date ignoring extensions; then any extension provisions, and either party’s right to terminate.</li>
    <li>Every affected title number, including provisional numbers.</li>
    <li>Location: nothing more if whole titles are affected; if only part, a Shapefile or GeoJSON in British National Grid is the current option. See <a href="/spatial-data/geojson-shapefile-british-national-grid/">spatial data</a>.</li>
    <li>Address, postcode and any UPRN, and whether separately held sub-surface land or airspace is included.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The full field-by-field breakdown is in <a href="/hmlr/information-required/">what HMLR will ask for</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="consequences">If you don’t report</h2>
  <p>Failing to provide the information without reasonable excuse, or knowingly or recklessly providing false or misleading information, is an offence under section 225 of the Levelling-up and Regeneration Act 2023. It can apply to companies, partnerships and the officers acting for them. HMLR has said that if you follow its guidance and reasonably conclude no information is needed, that may amount to a reasonable excuse.</p>
  <p>The commercial consequence lands sooner. HMLR will not generally enter a notice or restriction protecting a contractual control right until the information has been provided. Without that protection, an option may not bind a buyer of the land. See <a href="/compliance/penalties-and-consequences/">penalties and consequences</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="public">The 2028 dataset</h2>
  <p>HMLR expects to publish the contractual control dataset from April 2028 and keep it updated at least monthly, in a standard downloadable format. It will show where the land is and its extent, who holds the right, what type it is, how long it runs and when it was granted or exercised. For the first time, competitors, adjoining owners and planning authorities will be able to see a land team’s pipeline, and when each option expires. What that means for each side of the market is in <a href="/intelligence/what-the-2028-dataset-will-show/">what the dataset will show</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="now">What to do now</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>List every agreement signed since 8 June 2026.</strong> They are already caught. The deadline is 6 October 2027 and the service opens only six months before it.</li>
    <li><strong>Run each right through the tests.</strong> One line per right, not per contract.</li>
    <li><strong>Get the data ready.</strong> Company numbers, title numbers, period of control, extension and termination clauses, and a spatial file wherever only part of a title is affected.</li>
    <li><strong>Agree who submits.</strong> Name the individually regulated conveyancer for each right and how they will be instructed.</li>
    <li><strong>Diary the triggers.</strong> Every assignment, variation and exercise from 6 April 2027 starts a new 60-day clock, including on older agreements.</li>
    <li><strong>Review templates.</strong> Confidentiality clauses cannot stop HMLR publishing, so plan for visibility in negotiations.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>The <a href="/readiness-checklist/">readiness checklist</a> turns this into a working list you can print or save.</p>
</Guide>
